= Resistor — Simple =

A '''resistor''' is a small electronic component that makes it harder for electric current to flow. Engineers use resistors to limit current, divide voltage, and help circuits behave predictably.

== How it relates to information ==
A computer reads changing electrical signals. Resistors help produce safe, predictable voltages and can help a digital circuit distinguish its intended electrical states. They can also help measuring equipment convert a changing physical condition into a voltage that other electronics can read.

A resistor does not decide what information means and does not calculate by itself. It is part of the physical circuit that carries or measures a signal.

== Example ==
Two suitable resistors connected as a voltage divider can make a smaller voltage from a larger voltage. A sensor or measuring device can then read that voltage, provided the circuit is designed for the expected input and load.

== Limitations ==
Real resistors heat up and have tolerances; their values are not perfectly exact. Selecting a resistor requires attention to voltage, power and the circuit it connects to. The detailed electrical formulas are in the [[Resistor|Core]] and [[Resistor/Technical|Technical]] versions.
